# Insertion Sort - Part 1

## Problem Summary

Given an array where the first N-1 elements are already sorted, insert the last element into its correct position by shifting larger elements to the right.

## Example

Input:

5
2 4 6 8 3

The last element is 3.

The elements greater than 3 are shifted one position to the right until the correct position for 3 is found.

Final array:

2 3 4 6 8

## Approach

1. Store the last element as the value to be inserted.
2. Start comparing it with the element immediately before it.
3. If the previous element is greater, shift it one position to the right.
4. Continue moving toward the beginning of the array.
5. Insert the stored value into its correct position.
6. Print the array after every shift as required by HackerRank.

## Algorithm

Use the insertion-sort technique to shift elements greater than the last element toward the right and then insert the last element into the correct position.

## Time Complexity

O(N) for this Part 1 operation.

## Auxiliary Space Complexity

O(1)

## Why This Approach Is Efficient

The algorithm directly shifts only the elements that are greater than the value being inserted. It does not perform unnecessary sorting of the entire array.

## Alternative Approach

A general insertion sort can be used to sort the complete array. However, this problem only requires inserting the final element, so processing the entire array is unnecessary.

